es on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}+y\\sqrt{x}$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=-3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(1,-3,8)$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(1,-3).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(1,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,-3) & =\\frac{{1}^{2}+1}{{1}^{2}(-3)^{2}}+(-3)\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}-3(1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{25}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-132584/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "049e8586-8348-50be-ac56-4ea79eba43cd"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-132584"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-64968/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "44dc2923-43e6-5ba8-ba14-60d522a61684" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-64968/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "c0d599d8-7400-5fcb-84b4-4a9d937b2604" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-64968/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "a4b89cf8-7d79-56d5-8db2-3531bec087a5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-64968/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "74f41e2a-620e-5649-9430-5390670de978" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-64968/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "5eddbac7-927b-5213-85df-660169204d56" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-64968/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "17da8771-82c2-5ba7-b963-1f0b8cb7839e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-64968/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-64968/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-64968/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-64968/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-64968/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" "q-64968/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-64968" :question/id #uuid "8cde8878-d1f7-5e10-aeab-7b3b6336ac1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-64968" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=1.$ Given that the point $P$ with $y$-coordinate $0$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=\\sqrt{2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy+\\frac{\\pi }{4})$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point where $y=0$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(1,0).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(1,0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,0) & =\\sqrt{2}(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot 0+\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-64968/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "049e8586-8348-50be-ac56-4ea79eba43cd"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-64968"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-132579/selection/26d56282497397e2cb22481c4a4fa1a5e785860d52ba1416fe2e4e5146075126" :answer/id #uuid "9829bd35-dce6-543f-a40a-71578ce0965f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2-\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132579/selection/56479adfab14a04f0f6a8e2d1a4b4562282e5b877ddb2e1f85045a538e811394" :answer/id #uuid "7746ea2b-6e18-524f-b9cc-4542b80bad2f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1+2\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132579/selection/8642f29c07ed588847b818ca52d7d9b13f60105c8ca35b3f5cb1a649a54ead1c" :answer/id #uuid "e05f54fa-df5e-5702-b9e2-4bf0c3be2f0c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132579/selection/cad4ec6e5d618fc013e0e5d6d2ec5b534f59f168293077d41c9148a819b4e86c" :answer/id #uuid "7c56a0dc-54e8-5f1a-903d-61ac8b42e52c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1-\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132579/selection/6acadda2cf01d7393af5cf150300ca180189308ad197cceffca549e1b6e5368c" :answer/id #uuid "779e5211-b5b7-5136-a9da-f5f7f40f9240" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi -1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132579/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "7dc65800-a9b6-58fe-99c5-342a293c07c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-132579/selection/cad4ec6e5d618fc013e0e5d6d2ec5b534f59f168293077d41c9148a819b4e86c" :answer-field/choices ["q-132579/selection/26d56282497397e2cb22481c4a4fa1a5e785860d52ba1416fe2e4e5146075126" "q-132579/selection/56479adfab14a04f0f6a8e2d1a4b4562282e5b877ddb2e1f85045a538e811394" "q-132579/selection/8642f29c07ed588847b818ca52d7d9b13f60105c8ca35b3f5cb1a649a54ead1c" "q-132579/selection/cad4ec6e5d618fc013e0e5d6d2ec5b534f59f168293077d41c9148a819b4e86c" "q-132579/selection/6acadda2cf01d7393af5cf150300ca180189308ad197cceffca549e1b6e5368c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-132579" :question/id #uuid "6336c497-86b6-5ccd-aae1-b53920cad5d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-132579" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $y=2.$ Given that the point $P$ with $x$-coordinate $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+xy$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point where $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-1-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-132579/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "049e8586-8348-50be-ac56-4ea79eba43cd"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-132579"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-36301/selection/db07129af0a4f2d4881a52d025d8fb510ae618acefb24a92eb7f85fdeb1d3c19" :answer/id #uuid "b8627a81-a6be-5f51-833d-e2dac9e41cfe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=-4x\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-36301/selection/c993eb9175e9065e248d2fa7339675605c5aa5bbf2341711da8c44e3e10ccaf9" :answer/id #uuid "c28ea5e1-e812-56d9-a4c7-c4ac2e6be747" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=4y-8\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-36301/selection/c34b6c80c47390a1244010397ad26f857f46f06c91a444145aa0510a3a9f5c83" :answer/id #uuid "43a5c0a2-5e4e-52c9-a835-0d0ada2e39fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=4x+8\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-36301/selection/b825f30ce8eed011fba0d41ec34c23498c94618cd6ff40260d5812bf47fd7054" :answer/id #uuid "a39aaeb9-f462-5498-b89c-1c8c6a9c0619" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=-4y+8\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-36301/selection/a7897a7b427c3c81926b26a68e0ee94ebfb2015ad213ed59b4b66cd1cfbc6a0b" :answer/id #uuid "65d0729f-4778-51b8-a483-1909ab84edf0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=4y+8\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-36301/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "040cceeb-77af-5045-8aef-ab24f611ebe2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-36301/selection/b825f30ce8eed011fba0d41ec34c23498c94618cd6ff40260d5812bf47fd7054" :answer-field/choices ["q-36301/selection/db07129af0a4f2d4881a52d025d8fb510ae618acefb24a92eb7f85fdeb1d3c19" "q-36301/selection/c993eb9175e9065e248d2fa7339675605c5aa5bbf2341711da8c44e3e10ccaf9" "q-36301/selection/c34b6c80c47390a1244010397ad26f857f46f06c91a444145aa0510a3a9f5c83" "q-36301/selection/b825f30ce8eed011fba0d41ec34c23498c94618cd6ff40260d5812bf47fd7054" "q-36301/selection/a7897a7b427c3c81926b26a68e0ee94ebfb2015ad213ed59b4b66cd1cfbc6a0b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-36301" :question/id #uuid "33bf0daa-0427-5567-9a5c-6a79e37f5a37" :question/math-academy-id "q-36301" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=-2.$ Given that the point $P(-2,0,8)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+4y+x$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=-2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(-2,0,8)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(-2,0)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(-2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(-2,0)} & =(-2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+4(0)+(-2) \\\\  & =-2-2 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(-2,0)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(-2,0)}(y-0) \\\\ z-8 & =-4(y-0) \\\\ z & =-4y+8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,0,8)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=-4y+8\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-36301/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c5685f7-71b2-58ae-8c61-f555b355d92f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-36301"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-36441/selection/a1cf84bb5a83d574793306d1dc0979cc1e7866ae9934ac987d5c6fd2aab9ccf0" :answer/id #uuid "519b4415-c57e-53b7-9b0e-92b9be6c80bc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=0.5 \\\\ z=-4y+12\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-36441/selection/014cc8d6e203b53f3c0daf09ab8c48734dff55603cf1227fdc796648baf92d80" :answer/id #uuid "6da13587-3d3f-5470-a50e-7c5d0a75e2cd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=0.5 \\\\ z=4x+2\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-36441/selection/37acfa6272f55415b68c93278e2fb7547e9227368b2b44d0de1871a5e3ef7ec7" :answer/id #uuid "c4c0ea80-ede6-524b-9322-8123deb65dfa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=0.5 \\\\ z=4y-4\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-36441/selection/4394d1815724f7aba4e0f607246d475992e80ef12f5f87f50cc70429f5fa0b83" :answer/id #uuid "dc87c32f-2c60-5e05-a849-fd01a25f9b42" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=4x+2\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-36441/selection/13b7cef62476a79935367b813226d5851ca86e9c840ef2087b95a6c8cc8274ca" :answer/id #uuid "10da0c28-49aa-51b3-81e1-2d18d2f02d2e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=0.5 \\\\ z=4y-12\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-36441/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "3954d628-4d5d-527a-90e5-ef106121e032" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-36441/selection/37acfa6272f55415b68c93278e2fb7547e9227368b2b44d0de1871a5e3ef7ec7" :answer-field/choices ["q-36441/selection/a1cf84bb5a83d574793306d1dc0979cc1e7866ae9934ac987d5c6fd2aab9ccf0" "q-36441/selection/014cc8d6e203b53f3c0daf09ab8c48734dff55603cf1227fdc796648baf92d80" "q-36441/selection/37acfa6272f55415b68c93278e2fb7547e9227368b2b44d0de1871a5e3ef7ec7" "q-36441/selection/4394d1815724f7aba4e0f607246d475992e80ef12f5f87f50cc70429f5fa0b83" "q-36441/selection/13b7cef62476a79935367b813226d5851ca86e9c840ef2087b95a6c8cc8274ca"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-36441" :question/id #uuid "34072283-dfb3-5acf-99b2-73755015acf1" :question/math-academy-id "q-36441" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=0.5.$ Given that the point $P(0.5,2,4)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=y+2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+\\frac{1}{x}$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=0.5.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(0.5,2,4)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(0.5,2)}.$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(0.5,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(0.5,2)} & =2+2(2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡((0.5)(2))+\\frac{1}{0.5} \\\\  & =2+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(0.5,2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(0.5,2)}(y-2) \\\\ z-4 & =4(y-2) \\\\ z & =4y-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(0.5,2,4)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=0.5 \\\\ z=4y-4\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-36441/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c5685f7-71b2-58ae-8c61-f555b355d92f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-36441"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-132590/selection/bd06e503547b30e5f71e57545b2be69ea9c792ecad821f66758409f1b8c078a9" :answer/id #uuid "8fc76348-2713-5cee-ac94-bae3d3c969af" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=-2x+\\pi \\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132590/selection/169ecc938f720eaf67e23a8200e18b773357f4e69f82e9a8984a5ed55676bf3e" :answer/id #uuid "382f32da-fa62-598a-8373-1b07a14e418e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=x+2\\pi \\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132590/selection/94d0d19034cfd020107f4657533ba54df6ed56b2b4e4bd0a4b0314506e960bbc" :answer/id #uuid "a583929c-bb75-579d-95b1-bf46b4cd8f55" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=-2x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132590/selection/ad89c095acaa6f2e5fc121aea35af0422c4bcacf27127c5f9217ff94e01993e3" :answer/id #uuid "8234ef11-10ca-5149-9ad8-a86240cd9fe7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=x-\\pi \\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132590/selection/2cea13f49349a5be959cefab4f19ffaa635cff678b3d768396154d2addf1f80e" :answer/id #uuid "86c20059-5dfa-5ce1-8fe9-2a4c9cb54c49" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=2x-2\\pi \\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132590/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "95677b3e-be0f-51c7-aace-c7b0e0552362" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-132590/selection/bd06e503547b30e5f71e57545b2be69ea9c792ecad821f66758409f1b8c078a9" :answer-field/choices ["q-132590/selection/bd06e503547b30e5f71e57545b2be69ea9c792ecad821f66758409f1b8c078a9" "q-132590/selection/169ecc938f720eaf67e23a8200e18b773357f4e69f82e9a8984a5ed55676bf3e" "q-132590/selection/94d0d19034cfd020107f4657533ba54df6ed56b2b4e4bd0a4b0314506e960bbc" "q-132590/selection/ad89c095acaa6f2e5fc121aea35af0422c4bcacf27127c5f9217ff94e01993e3" "q-132590/selection/2cea13f49349a5be959cefab4f19ffaa635cff678b3d768396154d2addf1f80e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-132590" :question/id #uuid "760c6872-f52d-53e8-b7b3-88a52e70f20e" :question/math-academy-id "q-132590" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=2.$ Given that the point $P(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2,2\\pi )$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{xy}{\\pi }$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2,2\\pi )$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(-\\pi /2,2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(-\\pi /2,2)} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})(2)}{\\pi } \\\\  & =-1-1 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(-\\pi /2,2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(-\\pi /2,2)}(x-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\ z-2\\pi  & =-2(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ z & =-2x+\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2,2\\pi )$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=-2x+\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-132590/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c5685f7-71b2-58ae-8c61-f555b355d92f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-132590"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-132589/selection/7ae4e787fd5a71a710113c1d98710a727b185b3337ec3b6cb3bb20ae6a04d658" :answer/id #uuid "97721005-5ca9-5036-b701-78c3eff0d71d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=e \\\\ z=2y-1\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132589/selection/00740f5a791e077996ad54d3c94e41d037f674de7d8e1e79d73febef407901e3" :answer/id #uuid "991042b2-3d27-5a0b-a6e2-49d17fca0d50" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=e \\\\ z=2y-3\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132589/selection/bff2cb38f1d67baa213e66451d23eea5e3c98ef41397943c8f55b5cede62a629" :answer/id #uuid "eb6a2638-2da6-56b4-a918-890205bbd536" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=e \\\\ z=y+3\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132589/selection/3b7f27ceaee3c4c834055be64eeaa09487f53295d78e8cc65e7685f5a632bdaa" :answer/id #uuid "c0e823a8-b2dd-5432-a6dd-7787c3f4a712" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=e \\\\ z=y+1\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132589/selection/1b04403361c752e6cfe5a8472449a28359341f4537f73079f2c62fb06dfd9f57" :answer/id #uuid "fb86dcd4-62b0-54a2-818f-cde5b9bd4e8b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=e \\\\ z=y-2\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132589/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c0ade84a-e009-5373-9e20-c5f73c9de403" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-132589/selection/3b7f27ceaee3c4c834055be64eeaa09487f53295d78e8cc65e7685f5a632bdaa" :answer-field/choices ["q-132589/selection/7ae4e787fd5a71a710113c1d98710a727b185b3337ec3b6cb3bb20ae6a04d658" "q-132589/selection/00740f5a791e077996ad54d3c94e41d037f674de7d8e1e79d73febef407901e3" "q-132589/selection/bff2cb38f1d67baa213e66451d23eea5e3c98ef41397943c8f55b5cede62a629" "q-132589/selection/3b7f27ceaee3c4c834055be64eeaa09487f53295d78e8cc65e7685f5a632bdaa" "q-132589/selection/1b04403361c752e6cfe5a8472449a28359341f4537f73079f2c62fb06dfd9f57"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-132589" :question/id #uuid "442ef4cc-2e2a-5f75-9134-aec36af10017" :question/math-academy-id "q-132589" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=e.$ Given that the point $P(e,2,3)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\frac{{e}^{y}}{{x}^{2}}$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=e.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(e,2,3)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(e,2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(e,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(e,2) & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e-\\frac{{e}^{2}}{{e}^{2}} \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(e,2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(e,2)}(y-2) \\\\ z-3 & =1(y-2) \\\\ z & =y+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(e,2,3)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=e \\\\ z=y+1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-132589/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c5685f7-71b2-58ae-8c61-f555b355d92f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-132589"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-71218/selection/f0b7b9702b324a4d6ca523bfe039d87b7b525f85ed1a3f091e230230c2faaebe" :answer/id #uuid "1687ea78-571c-5420-b775-22dadb21cbac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=2\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71218/selection/5dd8f22088e0666da769a2e7f125ba8adca9acc614f15d21f9aaabef4de761d6" :answer/id #uuid "f6f50bb7-8a2a-5602-8722-ed0ffb4f477f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=x+2\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71218/selection/28d31d1051a602f42ff149a4b0a932fc55682baa0757057d7368cf6c5376ffbe" :answer/id #uuid "6626448e-5b4f-5f98-8a20-7682838c8c0e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=-2\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71218/selection/96bd1d808fc40f3c16f7d3a018f5e7851b3553067d2edffe1676794add1a03c6" :answer/id #uuid "11905ccd-6edb-5a46-b398-0390aea63059" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=-x+2\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71218/selection/8e7936a64fcbd8ddb76faab9bdcfe4d287e3a70a274035bed57f58badbd9c486" :answer/id #uuid "5aa8f47b-37f9-57d6-b89b-788e69c98aaa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=x-2\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71218/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "b5255667-5bb3-563c-b8ce-809e544e6d69" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-71218/selection/5dd8f22088e0666da769a2e7f125ba8adca9acc614f15d21f9aaabef4de761d6" :answer-field/choices ["q-71218/selection/f0b7b9702b324a4d6ca523bfe039d87b7b525f85ed1a3f091e230230c2faaebe" "q-71218/selection/5dd8f22088e0666da769a2e7f125ba8adca9acc614f15d21f9aaabef4de761d6" "q-71218/selection/28d31d1051a602f42ff149a4b0a932fc55682baa0757057d7368cf6c5376ffbe" "q-71218/selection/96bd1d808fc40f3c16f7d3a018f5e7851b3553067d2edffe1676794add1a03c6" "q-71218/selection/8e7936a64fcbd8ddb76faab9bdcfe4d287e3a70a274035bed57f58badbd9c486"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-71218" :question/id #uuid "76f63137-b52c-5f1c-a959-b140c362941f" :question/math-academy-id "q-71218" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(0,1,2)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(0,1,2)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(0,1)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(0,1)} & =(0)^{2}+(1)^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0+1) \\\\  & =1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(0,1)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(0,1)}(x-0) \\\\ z-2 & =1(x) \\\\ z & =x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(0,1,2)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-71218/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c5685f7-71b2-58ae-8c61-f555b355d92f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-71218"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186124893 :question/answer-fields 17592186122380] {:db/id "q-36371/selection/ma-v1/ee5efcfc6d05b6ff645c711c3657764b9906e2c64a403a8ef68efea21160c7fc/d926c04981e42fd5237af5d74fdb1545f7347b28bbbbc24eee21d830a071429e" :answer/id #uuid "0f796832-1cc1-509b-afb5-012ac545dbd5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨2,0,-3⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-36371/selection/ma-v1/ee5efcfc6d05b6ff645c711c3657764b9906e2c64a403a8ef68efea21160c7fc/9249ec6c3bfc45d506d5be5a051886ff4886559c8d9ee2ba1521c22fbcfe552a" :answer/id #uuid "def5f028-b86a-508a-a959-32f84b331d5a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨1,1,-2⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-36371/selection/ma-v1/ee5efcfc6d05b6ff645c711c3657764b9906e2c64a403a8ef68efea21160c7fc/7e77cb94d66f9f14a56da859536dad67a13cd546e06a90a85ba656fd3e964042" :answer/id #uuid "7a1f2e8c-9096-55e8-aa66-babb50da9455" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨1,0,6⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-36371/selection/ma-v1/ee5efcfc6d05b6ff645c711c3657764b9906e2c64a403a8ef68efea21160c7fc/593636e42c53a0d43753e03baf9e0e758207a502388d3b5623bdd3cb05d99f51" :answer/id #uuid "b4878389-0d6f-5500-8584-aa949ccb29ed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨-1,1,2⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-36371/selection/ma-v1/ee5efcfc6d05b6ff645c711c3657764b9906e2c64a403a8ef68efea21160c7fc/b20b883134125d1f3b25382fbc75ae9d4a8e391312a6a2c26cef555dbe916a04" :answer/id #uuid "8b1543de-fbee-57f0-840f-09b15cf5f206" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨2,0,3⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-36371/selection/ma-v1/ee5efcfc6d05b6ff645c711c3657764b9906e2c64a403a8ef68efea21160c7fc" :answer-field/id #uuid "9f2d675e-7e56-5e4b-80f8-ed798dcdf47d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-36371/selection/ma-v1/ee5efcfc6d05b6ff645c711c3657764b9906e2c64a403a8ef68efea21160c7fc/7e77cb94d66f9f14a56da859536dad67a13cd546e06a90a85ba656fd3e964042" :answer-field/choices ["q-36371/selection/ma-v1/ee5efcfc6d05b6ff645c711c3657764b9906e2c64a403a8ef68efea21160c7fc/d926c04981e42fd5237af5d74fdb1545f7347b28bbbbc24eee21d830a071429e" "q-36371/selection/ma-v1/ee5efcfc6d05b6ff645c711c3657764b9906e2c64a403a8ef68efea21160c7fc/9249ec6c3bfc45d506d5be5a051886ff4886559c8d9ee2ba1521c22fbcfe552a" "q-36371/selection/ma-v1/ee5efcfc6d05b6ff645c711c3657764b9906e2c64a403a8ef68efea21160c7fc/7e77cb94d66f9f14a56da859536dad67a13cd546e06a90a85ba656fd3e964042" "q-36371/selection/ma-v1/ee5efcfc6d05b6ff645c711c3657764b9906e2c64a403a8ef68efea21160c7fc/593636e42c53a0d43753e03baf9e0e758207a502388d3b5623bdd3cb05d99f51" "q-36371/selection/ma-v1/ee5efcfc6d05b6ff645c711c3657764b9906e2c64a403a8ef68efea21160c7fc/b20b883134125d1f3b25382fbc75ae9d4a8e391312a6a2c26cef555dbe916a04"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-36371"] :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=-3.$ Given that the point $P(2,-3,3)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=6(x-1)$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=-3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(2,-3).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(2,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(2,-3) & =6(2-1)=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(2,-3) & ={f}_{x}(2,-3)(x-2) \\\\ z-3 & =6(x-2) \\\\ z-3 & =6x-12 \\\\ z & =6x-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,1,0)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-3 \\\\ z=6x-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=-3,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=6x-9$ (or $6x-z-9=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨6,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨6,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i-6\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-6⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-⟨-1,0,-6⟩=⟨1,0,6⟩$ is also parallel to it." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-36371/selection/ma-v1/ee5efcfc6d05b6ff645c711c3657764b9906e2c64a403a8ef68efea21160c7fc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-132599/selection/1bcb2fdfe136c211d7aaed44fb976bb11f8aa790f5ceb5878f3f6a915632de3d" :answer/id #uuid "f3ec3124-2d80-5025-bc2d-f74559ffd22b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨1,0,2⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132599/selection/1561cf37083a5b2f59fb91f55ede69c266ca3617ac03030aae8802d1cf669f26" :answer/id #uuid "d3795097-21ef-5069-87b3-fdaf4499687b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨1,0,5⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132599/selection/70683e561b5fdb4f9509665b1912e054a0318cd193719a435f7a458896e4bcee" :answer/id #uuid "eecb499d-01e5-5d99-9b67-13734354e5d4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨3,0,5⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132599/selection/c457855895bc1067a6194394000ebd060f4468538e77f0a1b68c4b2c254c1515" :answer/id #uuid "2ea7d46d-e3a5-557f-84fe-1637e2fcd61d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨3,0,1⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132599/selection/b20b883134125d1f3b25382fbc75ae9d4a8e391312a6a2c26cef555dbe916a04" :answer/id #uuid "b6753f10-77bc-5c44-8c29-30d9379f2cef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨2,0,3⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132599/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "70f31201-de1e-54e0-8303-6d62dd785868" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-132599/selection/1561cf37083a5b2f59fb91f55ede69c266ca3617ac03030aae8802d1cf669f26" :answer-field/choices ["q-132599/selection/1bcb2fdfe136c211d7aaed44fb976bb11f8aa790f5ceb5878f3f6a915632de3d" "q-132599/selection/1561cf37083a5b2f59fb91f55ede69c266ca3617ac03030aae8802d1cf669f26" "q-132599/selection/70683e561b5fdb4f9509665b1912e054a0318cd193719a435f7a458896e4bcee" "q-132599/selection/c457855895bc1067a6194394000ebd060f4468538e77f0a1b68c4b2c254c1515" "q-132599/selection/b20b883134125d1f3b25382fbc75ae9d4a8e391312a6a2c26cef555dbe916a04"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-132599" :question/id #uuid "55ea53d1-23d3-50b0-af4a-d002f4c94b27" :question/math-academy-id "q-132599" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(2,1,4)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}={e}^{y-1}+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y-1)$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(2,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(2,1) & ={e}^{1-1}+2(2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1-1) \\\\  & =1+4 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(2,1) & ={f}_{x}(2,1)(x-2) \\\\ z-4 & =5(x-2) \\\\ z & =5x-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(2,1,4)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=5x-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=5x-6$ (or $5x-z-6=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨5,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨5,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i-5\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-5⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-⟨-1,0,-5⟩=⟨1,0,5⟩$ is also parallel to it." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-132599/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0325887-ce25-5d6f-bf27-d85541e1dbe0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-132599"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-71220/selection/b55fab1eb102a5d57c0a5a2e0c55108292008db52428694c418a63484d580176" :answer/id #uuid "81541cd4-92b4-580d-94a8-5fc9d7efa355" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨0,1,1⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71220/selection/546883b9441c5fb3a054379dab9c65355fde2da3d83c5d3f7f0727159514b942" :answer/id #uuid "ea12e558-b041-5760-8e95-5223f775f3a9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨0,-2,1⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71220/selection/b80244a49f32f254bcf6e1e9dac54ecd97f129c2864d99d84704519dc914ddff" :answer/id #uuid "029a2e0e-0f4e-51ca-a7f3-34f2594d5f9f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨0,6,1⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71220/selection/43ffda529e765eee7ba335a7be277b97279a2e5469f5f58597ed2200aa4220d6" :answer/id #uuid "991daaff-2d46-56b2-9cb6-3ae09a927011" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨0,-1,1⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71220/selection/0612990914a9c0d10ccca2300c033375b7f6bebaa36396ac813d2f53d34698ce" :answer/id #uuid "cf0840cc-8f40-554d-aa27-2ab328a01c38" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨0,-6,1⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71220/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "cf8da1d4-0854-5f96-a089-243dfb234ab5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-71220/selection/43ffda529e765eee7ba335a7be277b97279a2e5469f5f58597ed2200aa4220d6" :answer-field/choices ["q-71220/selection/b55fab1eb102a5d57c0a5a2e0c55108292008db52428694c418a63484d580176" "q-71220/selection/546883b9441c5fb3a054379dab9c65355fde2da3d83c5d3f7f0727159514b942" "q-71220/selection/b80244a49f32f254bcf6e1e9dac54ecd97f129c2864d99d84704519dc914ddff" "q-71220/selection/43ffda529e765eee7ba335a7be277b97279a2e5469f5f58597ed2200aa4220d6" "q-71220/selection/0612990914a9c0d10ccca2300c033375b7f6bebaa36396ac813d2f53d34698ce"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-71220" :question/id #uuid "daf286ba-b2e7-5950-80df-28aebe5b694f" :question/math-academy-id "q-71220" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=-2.$ Given that the point $P(-2,-6,1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=\\frac{y-x}{\\sqrt{x(y+x)}}$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=-2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(-2,-6).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(-2,-6):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-2,-6) & =\\frac{-6-(-2)}{\\sqrt{(-2)(-6+(-2))}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{\\sqrt{16}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{4} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-2,-6) & ={f}_{y}(-2,-6)(y-(-6)) \\\\ z-1 & =-1(y+6) \\\\ z-1 & =-y-6 \\\\ z & =-y-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,-6,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=-y-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=-2,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=-y-5$ (or $y+z+5=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,1,1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-j+k \\\\  & =⟨0,-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,-1,1⟩.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-71220/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0325887-ce25-5d6f-bf27-d85541e1dbe0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-71220"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-71221/selection/8ea36c6403429ca3852994b1b8427fc97855373b375abb3c7d005c9eca8bd9bd" :answer/id #uuid "3fc2261e-3cfa-5b95-91ca-7c836dd00b9b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨1,0,3⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71221/selection/6ac8d68cd3c81cbc09c5173f581c5cba3c0c9b1bc976b14a17f80056899d3927" :answer/id #uuid "e513b108-d128-5817-8929-cdc0a6e85a63" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨-1,0,3⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71221/selection/eca61f1b84786d16ad626af7ba601bb554c91c7a8e27048a60a489599617710e" :answer/id #uuid "c482fcda-47fb-577a-af30-3864401afb96" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨1,0,1⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71221/selection/c457855895bc1067a6194394000ebd060f4468538e77f0a1b68c4b2c254c1515" :answer/id #uuid "632bb2b6-7e80-5acf-a561-337f21f6ce9e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨3,0,1⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71221/selection/3b2ce7e3af392e572e181f04f8cd0bb84efd17cf0615829c947630b689b2f861" :answer/id #uuid "3412a480-0907-594d-975b-67c1d5a0181e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨1,0,-3⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71221/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "821bc836-4412-5373-8984-9c9b1100abd2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-71221/selection/8ea36c6403429ca3852994b1b8427fc97855373b375abb3c7d005c9eca8bd9bd" :answer-field/choices ["q-71221/selection/8ea36c6403429ca3852994b1b8427fc97855373b375abb3c7d005c9eca8bd9bd" "q-71221/selection/6ac8d68cd3c81cbc09c5173f581c5cba3c0c9b1bc976b14a17f80056899d3927" "q-71221/selection/eca61f1b84786d16ad626af7ba601bb554c91c7a8e27048a60a489599617710e" "q-71221/selection/c457855895bc1067a6194394000ebd060f4468538e77f0a1b68c4b2c254c1515" "q-71221/selection/3b2ce7e3af392e572e181f04f8cd0bb84efd17cf0615829c947630b689b2f861"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-71221" :question/id #uuid "23da9fa3-3fe5-5812-be11-6d830c793b0c" :question/math-academy-id "q-71221" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(-1,1,-1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=3{e}^{x+y}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|xy|$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(-1,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(-1,1) & =3{e}^{-1+1}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(-1)\\cdot 1| \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\  & =3-0 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-1,1) & ={f}_{x}(-1,1)(x-(-1)) \\\\ z-(-1) & =3(x+1) \\\\ z+1 & =3x+3 \\\\ z & =3x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-1,1,-1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=3x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=3x+2$ (or $3x-z+2=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨3,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨3,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i-3\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-3⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-⟨-1,0,-3⟩=⟨1,0,3⟩$ is also parallel to it." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-71221/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0325887-ce25-5d6f-bf27-d85541e1dbe0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-71221"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-64971/selection/97238b42b3423f6af8d312d76b8ae4f7c7dadb6f5362562803e7e3b2c25fa180" :answer/id #uuid "d72b8b3f-ed70-5049-83ed-e150e1e0ecfb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "i-2\\,k" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-64971/selection/fc0739e66cc34ae50c9c0c2f36204c8bfd332359e19d40120dc00d407c8db40d" :answer/id #uuid "ae641c86-c39b-5f04-9d92-346f62d73bd1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2i-2\\,k" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-64971/selection/0d1a257411723a897b11cb8c0a6bf2613f465da900af3d790a2855f89a5b4324" :answer/id #uuid "3e671d02-3be9-599f-8498-1f0470cf8072" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "i+2\\,k" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-64971/selection/15da83457437188e0bfca47aef73f012cf281e3a0a3dd69166188fad952ada23" :answer/id #uuid "8cc02da0-c8a4-5fd0-aace-6fae8e8f22f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3i+\\,k" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-64971/selection/69693a012948bc753e7039a20ed6d6cc563c78c16921b9747632deed0f78c33e" :answer/id #uuid "88a9c96f-283f-59b6-8c97-cb2d710d23f3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "i+\\,k" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-64971/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "e1303ce5-14a0-59bc-a030-ea7ee71a4f4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-64971/selection/0d1a257411723a897b11cb8c0a6bf2613f465da900af3d790a2855f89a5b4324" :answer-field/choices ["q-64971/selection/97238b42b3423f6af8d312d76b8ae4f7c7dadb6f5362562803e7e3b2c25fa180" "q-64971/selection/fc0739e66cc34ae50c9c0c2f36204c8bfd332359e19d40120dc00d407c8db40d" "q-64971/selection/0d1a257411723a897b11cb8c0a6bf2613f465da900af3d790a2855f89a5b4324" "q-64971/selection/15da83457437188e0bfca47aef73f012cf281e3a0a3dd69166188fad952ada23" "q-64971/selection/69693a012948bc753e7039a20ed6d6cc563c78c16921b9747632deed0f78c33e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-64971" :question/id #uuid "2a3ebb85-3220-5af9-9101-50c80b1e42cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-64971" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,1,1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=2(x+y)-2$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(1,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,1) & =2(1+1)-2=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(1,1) & ={f}_{x}(1,1)(x-1) \\\\ z-1 & =2(x-1) \\\\ z-1 & =2x-2 \\\\ z & =2x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,1,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=2x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=2x-1$ (or $2x-z-1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨2,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨2,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i-2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $-i-2\\,k$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-(-i-2\\,k)=i+2\\,k$ is also parallel to it." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-64971/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0325887-ce25-5d6f-bf27-d85541e1dbe0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-64971"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3068"] :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=2.$ Given that the point $P(2,1,8)$ lies on $C,$ and that $f_y = \\dfrac{4x}y - 4x^2y,$ find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=2.$ So, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(2,1,8)$ will be given by the partial derivative $f_y(2,1).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $f_y(x,y)$ at $(2,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(2,1) & =\\frac{4(2)}{(1)}-4(2)^{2}(1) \\\\  & =8-16 \\\\  & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3069"] :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=\\pi.$ Given that the point $P(1,\\pi,-1)$ lies on $C,$ and that $f_x = y\\cos(xy) + \\cos y,$ find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z = f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=\\pi.$ So, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(1,\\pi,-1)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\dfrac {\\partial z} {\\partial x} \\Bigg|_{(1,\\pi)}.$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\dfrac {\\partial z} {\\partial x}$ at $(1,\\pi)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,\\pi )} & =\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot \\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi  \\\\  & =\\pi (-1)+(-1) \\\\  & =-\\pi -1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(1,\\pi )} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,\\pi )}(x-1) \\\\ z-(-1) & =(-\\pi -1)(x-1) \\\\ z+1 & =(-\\pi -1)x+\\pi +1 \\\\ z & =-(\\pi +1)x+\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above represents a line in the $xz$-plane. The $xz$-plane through the point $P(1,\\pi,-1)$ has the equation $y=\\pi.$\n\nSo, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,\\pi,-1)$ is given by the intersection of the following planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\pi  \\\\ z=-(\\pi +1)x+\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3070"] :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(0,1,1)$ lies on $C,$ and that $f_x =  \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2} \\pi}{4} \\sin{\\left(2 x + \\dfrac{\\pi y}{4} \\right)},$ find a tangent vector to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=1.$ So, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative $f_x(0,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $f_x(x,y)$ at $(0,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,1) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(0)+\\frac{\\pi (1)}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(0,1) & ={f}_{x}(0,1)(x-0) \\\\ z-1 & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}x \\\\ z & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above represents a line in the $xz$-plane. The $xz$-plane through the point $P(0,1,1)$ has the equation $y=1.$\n\nSo, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(0,1,1)$ is given by the intersection of the following planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=\\frac{\\pi }{4}x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find a tangent vector, we can find a normal vector to each plane and compute their cross product.\n\n- For the plane $y = 1,$ we write $0x + 1y + 0z = 1$ and find the normal vector $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 0,1,0 \\rangle.$\n\n- For the plane $z = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} x +1,$ we write $-\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} x + 0y + 1z = 1$ and find the normal vector $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\left\\langle -\\dfrac{\\pi}4,0,1 \\right\\rangle.$\n\nComputing the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨-\\frac{\\pi }{4},0,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\pi /4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =i+\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we've found a tangent vector, and this could be our final answer. However, just to make our answer a little nicer, we can multiply the vector by $4$ to yield a simpler, parallel vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot (i+\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,k)=4\\,i+\\pi \\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
